00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
today's video has been sponsored by  ubisoft  i'm always hesitant when i approach  movies and games that are set in some  kind of mythological world  because a lot of the time it feels like  an afterthought  it's also rather difficult to find the  balance between paying homage to the  original stories  and still branching out and creating  something new  when ubisoft approached me about  discussing immortals phoenix rising  a new action-adventure game rooted in  greek mythology  i was definitely intrigued i had no idea  what to expect but i was more than happy  to give it a try  after playing phoenix rising for about  five minutes it was pretty clear this  was a game and story  designed with greek mythology at its  very core  if i'm being honest i thought i'd find  myself nitpicking the narrative and the  way the characters were portrayed  but there wasn't much i disagreed with  it was definitely an  encouraging start knowing that the team  behind this clearly cared about the  source material  for those intrigued about the story i'll  give you a brief summary without any  major spoilers  the game begins with a monologue from  typhon who tells us he's going to  destroy the gods and start a new world  those of you familiar with greek  mythology will know typhon  is the closest the greek gods ever came  to being destroyed  along with some other attempts from the  giants and the tysons  so having typhon as the big bad villain  of this game  is something i wholeheartedly agree with  you'd need an antagonist the gods are  scared of  and that can only really be typhon  having defeated the gods and heroes  there is no hope left  as even the humans have all been turned  to stone barring one of course  who washes up on the shores of the  golden isle it is down to you  phoenix to reunite the gods and stop  typhoon destroying  everything one of my favorite parts of  the game that does bring up  multiple points of discussion is the  relationship between  zeus and prometheus who serve as the  primary narrators of the game  as well as helping to establish an  overall tone  zeus and prometheus have somewhat of a  troubled history  which began with prometheus stealing  fire and giving it to mankind  zeus not being a big fan of humans then  chains him to a mountain where an  eagle would swoop down and devour his  liver only for it to regenerate in the  evening and the process to repeat itself  given the situation you'd expect a  rather large amount of animosity between  them  but that's not really the case as both  characters offer comedic relief as well  as guiding you through an  unknown world after typhon's monologue  were taken to the mountain where  prometheus is chained  and he tells zeus of a prophecy a human  mortal will unite the gods and defeat  typhon zeus naturally scoffs at the idea  and even wages prometheus his freedom if  he is correct  and so begins the split narration as you  journey through the golden isle  prometheus narrates the bulk of your  story  and zeus just does zeus things most of  the time he interrupts to be an a-hole  which is a very zeus thing to do the two  do a very good job explaining  stories that relate to whatever quest  you may be doing but it doesn't feel  like a constant  dump of exposition sometimes they'll  start an explanation  only to go completely off topic and  start arguing with each other over  something ridiculously petty  i really enjoyed the constant banter  between the two it gave the game its own  charm  and it showed a good deal of  self-awareness because trying to make a  game based on greek mythology  in a completely serious tone with no  humor  is just the wrong approach considering  how weird and wacky greek mythology can  be at times it may work for a game  such as god of war where the gods exist  just to be butchered by kratos so  there's no need to get to know them  here however phoenix and the gods are on  the same side  so you need the gods to be relatable or  have some redeeming qualities  otherwise you'll just end up rooting for  typhon  to say that immortals is full of jokes  and puns  is the understatement of the year you  can't go five minutes without someone  making a joke or just  doing something comical a good portion  of this comes from zeus and hermes  who for me are big highlights of this  game some people  may not like that approach but i do  having a witty and light-hearted tone  suits the personalities of most of the  gods  it also gives the game a more fun and  chill vibe  as opposed to being stressed and anxious  all the time which isn't what i  personally want from a game that offers  so much story and  exploration the exploration and world  design  is something that really surprised me  when you hear a game tell you  it's open world normally that just means  vast  open spaces that all look the same and  often feel quite empty  phoenix rising doesn't try to make its  world feel vast for the sake of it  instead it's quite a compact map it's  not particularly large in comparison to  other games  but it is absolutely jam-packed of  things to do  at no point did i find myself running  from a to b  for hours with nothing to do along the  way there is always some kind of  creature  collectible quest or event to take part  in  each zone has its own distinct look with  four of them representing a specific  deity  athena ares aphrodite and hephaestus  you can walk into a zone and within a  few seconds of just looking around  you can tell which god's domain you've  entered  the game also doesn't hold your hand  when you enter a zone there is no quest  log  you have to find the point of interest  and solve any puzzles along the way by  yourself  as it should be also i haven't mentioned  it yet but the game just looks really  nice  which makes you want to explore it  definitely has that stylized breath of  the wild feel to it while still  maintaining its own  vibrant aesthetic moving swiftly on to  the enemies and mythical creatures you  encounter  as typhon has already ravaged much of  the world most of the human soldiers you  encounter have been brought back as  shades  and this is the case for the greek  heroes also you encounter the likes of  achilles heracles odysseus  and atalanta but they aren't quite  themselves  and it's up to you to free them from  typhon's hold  as for mythical creatures if it exists  in greek mythology  then it's probably in this game i've  come across everything from  harpies gorgons cyclopes griffins  minotaurs chimera hydra hekatonkries and  even cerberus each creature has their  own unique feel and characteristics  each time i heard a high-pitched shriek  i knew it could only be a  wretched harpy and i took way too much  pleasure in guiding my arrows into their  disgusting bird face before they were  plucked out the sky  not a huge fan of harpies if you didn't  guess the combat system in this game as  a whole  isn't very hard and it's pretty fun once  you've mastered most of the mechanics  you can deal with these creatures  pretty easily that being said when  you've just started some of these  creatures can be daunting on the harder  difficulties  i remember leaving the tutorial portion  of the game and picking a fight with the  first cyclops i found  which wasn't the best choice considering  i had no idea what i was doing  after being thrown around with a slither  of hp and no pomegranates to heal  i had only one choice i had to find the  cheesiest way to kill this cyclops  because i was not about to be bested by  the first one-eyed big dumb dumb i came  across  so i did what any fearless warrior would  do in my position  i hid behind a rock and fired arrows at  him for about five to ten minutes until  he died  i don't know if the ai in this case was  just bad or if the cyclops ai is  designed to be dumb  but it felt like an authentic encounter  with a cyclops  this is something that happens  throughout the game your interactions  with creatures  gods and even the environment just have  an authentic  feel immortal certainly puts its own  spin on greek mythology  but never does it feel as if these  stories have been diluted or changed to  fit the narrative  it's clear the narrative was built  around these stories and legends  whether you're well versed in greek  mythology or someone who knows very  little  immortals does its best to appease both  audiences  although i didn't necessarily learn  anything new that didn't really affect  my enjoyment of the game  i constantly found myself listening for  references and searching for easter eggs  which  there is no shortage of in this game on  the opposite  end if you're someone who knows very  little by the end of the game that won't  be the case anymore  as far as gateways into greek mythology  go phoenix rising does a really good job  if you enjoy greek mythology and you  just want to relax and play something  fun that has a touch of nostalgia then i  recommend phoenix rising  if you've always wanted to dive into the  world of greek mythology but didn't know  where to start  then give phoenix rising a try  you

